      "classification": "vulnerability",
      "code_path": "src/index.ts::corsOptions::L23-31",
      "proof_trace": "`credentials: true` is hardcoded (line 29) while `origin` falls back to `'*'` when `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` is unset (lines 24-26), directly violating the assertion. The `cors` npm package emits both `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` and `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` simultaneously — an invalid combination per CORS spec — and no startup guard or env validation prevents this default-insecure state. The `.env.example` comment explicitly documents wildcard as the fallback ('未設定の場合は全て許可'), confirming this is the default production behavior when `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` is omitted.",
      "attack_scenario": "When deployed without `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` set, any cross-origin page can read responses from unauthenticated endpoints (e.g., `/health` leaking Discord guild ID, bot user tag, WebSocket status) without restriction; additionally, non-browser clients (embedded WebViews, server-side SSRF proxies) are not bound by browser CORS enforcement and can make credentialed cross-origin requests to all API endpoints.",
      "checklist_id": "PROP-stem-bot-v21-inv-004"
    },
    "text": "CORS origin must not default to wildcard '*' when credentials are enabled.",
    "assertion": "cors.credentials == true => cors.origin != '*'",
    "covers": "SG-018_corsSetup",
    "severity": "HIGH",
    "type": "invariant"
  }
}

